n></span></span></span></span>","enumerator":"2","key":"KG3KACm3FR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"It turns out that the exact probability in this case (using software) is 0.1316, so the approximation is excellent.","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"UDFZWy9mUT"}],"key":"FGG3z5ootg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The purpose of the next activity is to give you guided practice in solving word problems involving a binomial random variable, when the normal approximation is appropriate and is extremely helpful.","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"Pj7j0Gi5Dp"}],"key":"FfLmAq7s4s"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Applying the Continuity Correction","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"tCQWLEhpsK"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-applying-the-continuity-correction","label":"Check Your Understanding: Applying the Continuity Correction","html_id":"check-your-understanding-applying-the-continuity-correction","implicit":true,"key":"XIsUaALhea"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Roughly 10% of all college students in the United States are left-handed. Most academic institutions, therefore, try to have at least a few left-handed chairs in each classroom. 225 students are about to enter a lecture hall that has 30 left-handed chairs for a lecture. What is the probability that this is not going to be enough; in other words, what is the probability that more than 30 (or at least 31) of the 225 students are left-handed?","position":{"start":{"line":31,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"key":"o6yRgrpP2Z"}],"key":"f5KCPiO9yW"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let’s think about this situation.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"key":"DvHrhAVhQ1"}],"key":"nSjmRVTYr5"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Let X be the number of left-handed students (success) out of the 225 students (trials). X is therefore binomial with n = 225 and p = 0.1. We are asked to find P(X > 30) or P(X ≥ 31).","position":{"start":{"line":35,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"key":"A0WJRSFopt"}],"key":"HewN0OOvKM"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Clearly, doing this using the binomial distribution formula is out of the question.","position":{"start":{"line":37,"column":1},"end":{"line":37,"column":1}},"key":"vHqJyjfuSX"}],"key":"C1SNmSS9hW"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30b7ab02eb92796a87df491e36b218c4.mjs","model":{"question":"Is the normal approximation appropriate here, and if so, what are the mean and standard deviation of the approximating normal variable?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"Yes; μ = 22.5 and σ = 4.5","feedback":"Correct! np = 22.5 and n(1 − p) = 202.5 both exceed 10, so the approximation applies with μ = 22.5 and σ = √20.25 = 4.5."},{"correct":false,"text":"No; np is less than 10","feedback":"np = 225 × 0.1 = 22.5, which does satisfy the rule of thumb."},{"correct":false,"text":"Yes; μ = 22.5 and σ = 20.25","feedback":"The standard deviation is √(np(1−p)) = √20.25 = 4.5, not 20.25 (that's the variance)."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Check np and n(1 − p), then compute μ = np and σ = √(np(1 − p)).","explanation":""},"key":"x39OdTTJ5r"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-30b7ab02eb92796a87df491e36b218c4.mjs","model":{"question":"Using the normal approximation with continuity correction, what is P(X ≥ 31), the probability of running out of left-handed chairs?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"About 0.04","feedback":"Correct! P(Z ≥ 1.78) = P(Z ≤ −1.78) = 0.0375—about a 4% chance the 30 chairs won't be enough."},{"correct":false,"text":"About 0.96","feedback":"0.9625 is the probability that the chairs WILL be enough."},{"correct":false,"text":"1.78","feedback":"Standardize first: (30.5 − 22.5)/4.5 = 1.78; then find the tail probability from the table."}],"multi":false,"hint":"With the correction, use P(X ≥ 30.5): z = (30.5 − 22.5)/4.5 ≈ 1.78.","explanation":""},"key":"lHg3luFjlB"}],"key":"ymSyacWTH6"}],"key":"rHRyg4FDhD"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The Normal Approximation and When to Use It","url":"/pages/m11-normal-approximation-to-the-binomial-2-of-3","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"},"next":{"title":"Wrap-Up: Random Variables","url":"/pages/m11-wrap-up-random-variables","group":"Section 8: Random Variables"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}